# STAGE C - betting / opt-out. BASE is preserved verbatim as the first sentence
# block so the coordination task the model is betting ON is the same task Stage A
# measured; only the decision layer is new.
def betting_prompt(safe):
    # "The other player always plays" is load-bearing, not flavour. Every EV
    # benchmark in the analysis uses Stage A's UNCONDITIONAL self-play rate as the
    # match probability, and that is only the right p if the partner is not itself
    # selecting into the game. If the partner could also opt out, the payoff-relevant
    # quantity would be a cross-match against attempters only, and Stage A would be
    # measuring the wrong thing. Stating it removes the ambiguity rather than leaving
    # each trial to assume one way or the other.
    return (BASE + "\n\n"
            f"Before playing, you may either ATTEMPT the coordination game or take a "
            f"guaranteed SAFE payoff. The other player always plays the coordination "
            f"game and picks a form in the normal way; only you face this choice, and "
            f"the other player's choice does not depend on it. If you ATTEMPT and you "
            f"and the other player select the same underlying form, you receive 10 "
            f"points. If you ATTEMPT and mismatch, you receive 0 points. If you choose "
            f"SAFE, you receive {safe} points for certain and do not play. Choose the "
            f"option you prefer."
            "\n\nGive one short sentence of reasoning, then output exactly two lines:\n"
            "DECISION: <ATTEMPT or SAFE>\n"
            "CHOICE: <LEFT or RIGHT - the position in YOUR image of the shape you would "
            "pick; write CHOICE: NONE if you chose SAFE>")


